WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.024
Embedded security testing should include
more than the normal path.

2
00:00:04.104 --> 00:00:07.507
Test boundary inputs, malformed messages,
update failure cases,

3
00:00:07.587 --> 00:00:12.083
restart behavior, power interruption,
storage-full conditions, and recovery paths.

4
00:00:12.163 --> 00:00:14.289
These are realistic product conditions,

5
00:00:14.369 --> 00:00:16.921
not unusual edge cases to postpone
indefinitely.

6
00:00:17.001 --> 00:00:21.315
Static analysis, compiler warnings, stack
analysis, sanitizers where available,

7
00:00:21.395 --> 00:00:24.433
and hardware-in-the-loop tests can support
code quality.

8
00:00:24.513 --> 00:00:27.126
Different tools catch different classes of
issue,

9
00:00:27.206 --> 00:00:29.272
and not every tool works on every target.

10
00:00:29.352 --> 00:00:33.666
The practical goal is to combine tools
with design review and target-like evidence.

11
00:00:33.746 --> 00:00:38.121
Robustness testing can support parsers,
protocols, and file formats at a high level.

12
00:00:38.201 --> 00:00:41.664
The focus should be on finding crashes,
assertion failures, hangs,

13
00:00:41.744 --> 00:00:45.815
unexpected state transitions, and resource
leaks in a controlled development

14
00:00:45.895 --> 00:00:46.381
setting.

15
00:00:46.461 --> 00:00:49.742
Results should feed back into simpler
parsers, clearer limits,

16
00:00:49.822 --> 00:00:51.219
and better error handling.

17
00:00:51.299 --> 00:00:54.824
Code review should cover memory safety,
input validation, secrets,

18
00:00:54.904 --> 00:00:58.731
debug interfaces, update behavior,
logging, and product configuration.

19
00:00:58.811 --> 00:01:01.242
Reviewers should trace normal and error
paths.

20
00:01:01.322 --> 00:01:04.421
They should ask who owns each buffer,
which input is trusted,

21
00:01:04.501 --> 00:01:08.207
how failure is reported, and whether
release settings match the design.

22
00:01:08.287 --> 00:01:12.297
Lifecycle practices matter because
embedded products live in the real world.

23
00:01:12.377 --> 00:01:15.719
SBOM awareness, dependency tracking,
vulnerability response,

24
00:01:15.799 --> 00:01:19.931
update planning, credential rotation,
field diagnostics, service workflows,

25
00:01:20.011 --> 00:01:24.081
and end-of-life decisions all affect
whether a product remains maintainable.

26
00:01:24.161 --> 00:01:27.929
Security should be built into development,
manufacturing, deployment,

27
00:01:28.009 --> 00:01:30.135
maintenance, and retirement processes.

28
00:01:30.215 --> 00:01:33.253
A product that is easy to build but hard
to update, diagnose,

29
00:01:33.333 --> 00:01:36.128
or retire safely has an operational
security problem.

30
00:01:36.208 --> 00:01:39.672
The engineering process should support the
full life of the device.

31
00:01:39.752 --> 00:01:42.182
The strongest teams make evidence
repeatable.

32
00:01:42.262 --> 00:01:46.576
They keep review checklists current,
preserve test results, document exceptions,

33
00:01:46.656 --> 00:01:49.512
and connect device diagnostics to support
procedures.

34
00:01:49.592 --> 00:01:53.845
That discipline turns embedded security
from a release-time scramble into normal

35
00:01:53.925 --> 00:01:55.140
engineering practice.
